  "content": "**Updated cheatsheet**\n\n### 1) Rebuild the task spec cumulatively, not locally\nTreat every new user message as a potential constraint update. Reconstruct the full task specification from all user turns before judging the assistant, and revalidate earlier assumptions whenever later disclosures add, refine, or contradict facts. Don\u2019t evaluate against a partial earlier version once the user has supplied a more complete one.\n\n### 2) Prefer explicit user facts over inferred placeholders\nWhen the user gives a concrete value, correction, or clarifying relation, it replaces earlier guesses, placeholders, or derived assumptions. Collapse unresolved quantities as soon as the missing information appears; do not continue treating them as unknown if the user has already resolved them. A later correction should reset the affected subproblem rather than layering on top of prior speculation.\n\n### 3) Separate stated facts from inferred structure\nDistinguish what the user explicitly said from what the assistant inferred. If the user names a subset, remainder, reference quantity, or comparative relation, keep that relation explicit instead of flattening it into a single count. Common failure mode: the assistant silently converts an \u201cof the remainder / of the people who are X / twice as many as Y\u201d statement into an unrelated standalone quantity.\n\n### 4) Look for partition, remainder, and overlap signals\nPhrases like \u201cremaining,\u201d \u201cof those,\u201d \u201csubset of,\u201d \u201ctwice as many as,\u201d or \u201ctogether\u201d often signal that quantities are linked by inclusion, complement, or aggregation. The analyzer should verify whether the assistant respected those links or prematurely treated one group as the whole. Check conservation across groups: do the parts still sum or reconcile with the total after all user disclosures?\n\n### 5) Detect contradiction-by-assumption\nFlag it when the assistant invents a hidden subtype split, workaround, or interpretation that the user never confirmed, especially if later user text provides an explicit aggregate or total-time statement that makes the invented split unnecessary or wrong. If the assistant fixes an ambiguity on its own while the user\u2019s wording allows multiple readings, mark that as risky or incorrect rather than accepted.\n\n### 6) Track \u201cwhat is solved\u201d vs \u201cwhat remains unaccounted for\u201d\nA correct intermediate result can still be incomplete. The analyzer should preserve valid subresults while explicitly checking whether later user turns introduced additional required pieces that were never incorporated into the final synthesis. Use this to catch cases where the assistant stops after one subgroup, one stage of a process, or an intermediate total.\n\n### 7) Recompute from the full accumulated state when later turns change the denominator or target\nIf a later user message modifies the base quantity, reference group, or denominator of a percentage/ratio/total calculation, the earlier arithmetic path may no longer apply. Re-evaluate the final answer from the original full task state, not from an intermediate remainder or already-partial total that has been superseded.\n\n### 8) Diagnose stale problem framing\nA common failure is that the assistant answers the earlier version of the problem even after the user has updated it. Check whether the assistant\u2019s latest reasoning is anchored to an obsolete interpretation, especially in sharded conversations where the user adds missing context over multiple turns.\n\n### 9) Distinguish contextual flavor from operative constraints\nRole descriptions, story details, and scene-setting text may be irrelevant unless they change the requested output or constraints. Do not let narrative context override direct user instructions, numerical relationships, or explicit corrections. Use context only when it genuinely affects the task spec.\n\n### 10) Build compact, actionable diagnostics\nWhen flagging an issue, identify the missing linkage, missing constraint, or wrong interpretation that changed the outcome. Prefer \u201ccorrect subresult, incomplete final synthesis\u201d or \u201cignored later correction to the reference quantity\u201d over vague labels. The best diagnosis tells the next step what state must be restored, not how to solve the underlying task.\n\n### 11) For rewrite/compaction steps, keep only validated state\nWhen preparing an S2-style compacted context, retain the confirmed totals, confirmed relations, and any unresolved references that still matter. Drop speculative branches and misleading intermediate conclusions, even if their local arithmetic was right, if they would anchor the next response to the wrong interpretation.\n\n### 12) Treat each new shard as refinement of one evolving problem\nIn multi-turn conversations, later user shards usually refine the same task rather than introducing separate tasks. Merge them into one evolving specification, then re-check whether the assistant\u2019s answer remains consistent with the updated state. If the new shard changes the meaning of an earlier number or comparison, the earlier conclusion should be reconsidered, not preserved by inertia.",
